  "notes": "All sets, reps, percentages and '+' (AMRAP) markers checked cell by cell against the '4 Day LP' tab of the author's spreadsheet (the '+' is in the cell number format). The 4-day version has no T1 OHP day, so the per-lift '1+' rule does not cover OHP; the sheet's general line (increase your TM if you completed all the reps) is the only guidance. Rest periods are not given in the source. Assistance areas per day are from the sheet ('Chest, Arms, Back', 'Legs, Abs', 'Arms, Other', 'Back, Abs'); the sheet says assistance uses bodybuilding sets/reps fitted to your needs, so the 3 x 8-15 here is a placeholder. sessionMinutes is an estimate.",
  "verification": {
    "verdict": "corrected",
    "checkedAgainst": [
      "https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1O0scKzbV22jNs3yYcNYymon4fsY7b7PQbWPjstorM4o",
      "https://thefitness.wiki/routines/nsuns-lp/"
    ],
    "changes": [
      "Front squat set 2 percent was the float artefact 0.44999999999999996; set to 0.45 (sheet formula H14*0.45).",
      "incrementKg.lower changed from 5 to 2.5: the sheet's TM rule is rep-based and identical for all lifts (2-3 reps on the 1+ set = +5 lb, about 2.5 kg); there is no 5 kg lower-body default in the source.",
      "Progression description: added the sheet's TM rounding (CEILING) and set-weight rounding (MROUND to 5 lb / 2.5 kg), noted the kg values are an approximate conversion, noted bench TM is driven by Thursday's 1+ set, and removed the reference to incline bench (not in the 4-day version).",
      "evidenceNote: replaced the 5-day bench volume figure with the 4-day count (26 bench-pattern sets plus 8 OHP sets per week).",
      "notes: recorded that rest periods are not in the source and listed the sheet's assistance areas.",
      "All exercises, day order (Mon/Tue/Thu/Fri), sets, reps, percentages, AMRAP flags (Mon bench 8+, T1 1+ and last sets 5+/5+/3+, no AMRAP on T2), TM = 90% of 1RM, progression table, absence of a deload rule, and isFree=true (public Google Sheet) were confirmed against the source unchanged. The original Reddit post could not be fetched (archive.is and Wayback blocked); the Fitness Wiki quotes the same progression rule."
    ]
